 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   Skeleton computation using the potential field method is the slowest of examined techniques, but preprocessing time is not a main issue for the use case [S3].
-*   Memory usage depends on storing the distance map and skeleton segments [S3].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   Tested on data sets of sizes $256^3$ to $512^3$ [S3].
-*   Successfully distinguishes blobby heart structures filled with contrast agents from occluding vessels and rib bones [S3].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Allows visual separation of objects with similar intensity but different shapes; more intuitive user interface than per-voxel methods; robust to moderate within-class deformation [S3].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires proper masking/pre-segmentation, which can be challenging; imperfect pre-segmentations cause instabilities in shape-skeletons; tubiness classifier reliability depends on skeleton quality (incomplete segments boost standard deviation); cavities inside pre-segmentation disturb skeleton computation [S3].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   When features have overlapping intensity ranges but distinct shapes (e.g., medical imaging with contrast agents) [S3].
-*   When an intuitive, feature-based classification is preferred over per-voxel histogram interpretation [S3].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   When high-quality manual segmentation is not feasible and windowing is insufficient [S3].
-*   When real-time performance is critical and potential field skeletonization is too slow [S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by skeleton computation (potential field method is slow) and distance map calculation. Skeleton normalization and merging are linear in the number of voxels/skeleton points. Shape analysis is linear in the number of surface voxels and pairs for convexity.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the volume, pre-segmentation mask, distance map, and skeleton data structures.
+*   **Measured Performance:** The potential field method is the slowest among examined skeletonization techniques but produces the cleanest and smoothest curve-skeletons [S3]. Preprocessing time is not a main issue for the use case [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Intuitive user interface; distinguishes features with overlapping intensities; robust to noise via normalization; handles curved longitudinal shapes [S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-segmentation; imperfect pre-segmentations cause instabilities; tubiness classifier reliability depends on skeleton quality; heavily branching skeletons cause problems for decomposition and merging [S3].
+*   **When to use:** When features have similar intensities but different shapes (e.g., vessels vs. bones); when intuitive shape-based classification is desired.
+*   **When not to use:** When real-time performance is critical and preprocessing is prohibitive; when pre-segmentation is impossible or highly inaccurate.
 
 ## Background
-The concept of axial description of shape (skeleton) was proposed in 2D by Blum [2]. Binford [1] proposed generalized cylinders in 3D. Cornea et al. [4, 5] introduced hierarchical curve-skeletons using repulsive force fields. The specific method of Shape-based Transfer Functions was proposed by Praßni et al. [S3]. It is described in reference [17] [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of axial description of shape (skeleton) was proposed in 2D by Blum [2]. Binford [1] proposed generalized cylinders in 3D. Cornea et al. [4, 5] introduced hierarchical curve-skeletons using repulsive force fields. The method was described by Praßni et al. [17] [S1, unofficial][S3].
 
